1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,350
Force Z: The Sinking of the
Prince of Wales and Repulse

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:08,017
On the 2nd of December 1941,
two of the most powerful

3
00:00:08,097 --> 00:00:13,250
warships in the world came up the
Johore Strait and anchored at Singapore,

4
00:00:13,330 --> 00:00:15,875
and the island thrilled to see them.

5
00:00:15,875 --> 00:00:19,043
They were the first battle
fleet ever based there,

6
00:00:19,123 --> 00:00:23,136
and they had been sent halfway
round the world for one purpose:

7
00:00:23,216 --> 00:00:26,075
to frighten Japan into
leaving Malaya alone.

8
00:00:26,075 --> 00:00:30,408
Eight days later both ships
were on the seabed off Kuantan,

9
00:00:30,488 --> 00:00:35,575
sunk by aircraft in under two hours,
and 840 of their men were dead.

10
00:00:35,575 --> 00:00:38,620
No shot from Singapore's
famous guns had been

11
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:42,439
fired in their defence,
because the guns faced the sea,

12
00:00:42,519 --> 00:00:44,950
and the blow had come from the air.

13
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:48,644
This is the story of the
deterrent that deterred nothing —

14
00:00:48,724 --> 00:00:51,182
and of the two wrecks, war graves both,

15
00:00:51,262 --> 00:00:53,800
that are still being fought over today.

16
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:58,017
The gesture came first, and it
was a politician's gesture as much

17
00:00:58,097 --> 00:01:03,437
as a sailor's. In the autumn of 1941, with
Japan pressing south through Indochina,

18
00:01:03,517 --> 00:01:07,470
Winston Churchill and his cabinet
settled on sending a small,

19
00:01:07,550 --> 00:01:11,172
modern striking force to
Singapore — a "fleet in being",

20
00:01:11,252 --> 00:01:15,350
whose mere presence, they reasoned,
would make Tokyo hesitate.

21
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:20,479
The force was to be built around the
new battleship HMS Prince of Wales,

22
00:01:20,559 --> 00:01:25,688
the battlecruiser HMS Repulse, and
the aircraft carrier HMS Indomitable,

23
00:01:25,768 --> 00:01:29,675
whose planes would be the
fleet's eyes and its shield.

24
00:01:29,675 --> 00:01:33,724
Prince of Wales was barely a
year old and already famous:

25
00:01:33,804 --> 00:01:37,781
in May 1941 she had fought
the Bismarck in the action in

26
00:01:37,861 --> 00:01:41,693
which HMS Hood was lost, and
in August she had carried

27
00:01:41,773 --> 00:01:45,975
Churchill across the Atlantic
to meet President Roosevelt.

28
00:01:45,975 --> 00:01:49,405
Repulse was her opposite
in almost everything —

29
00:01:49,485 --> 00:01:52,990
a ship of the First World
War, launched in 1916,

30
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:58,000
fast and handsome and thin-skinned,
a greyhound among battleships.

31
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,225
Then the plan lost its shield
before it had even sailed.

32
00:02:02,225 --> 00:02:06,679
On the 3rd of November 1941,
entering Kingston harbour

33
00:02:06,759 --> 00:02:11,125
in Jamaica on her way east,
Indomitable ran aground.

34
00:02:11,125 --> 00:02:13,792
She needed twelve days of repairs in an

35
00:02:13,872 --> 00:02:16,900
American dry dock, and
she never caught up.

36
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:19,890
Force Z would go to
Singapore with no carrier —

37
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:22,699
a fact known in London,
and accepted there,

38
00:02:22,779 --> 00:02:27,467
because the gesture was the point:
the ships had been publicly announced,

39
00:02:27,547 --> 00:02:30,225
and their arrival was itself the message.

40
00:02:30,225 --> 00:02:34,250
Tokyo read the message
and answered it in kind.

41
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:38,747
When Churchill let it be known that
the two capital ships were coming,

42
00:02:38,827 --> 00:02:43,324
Japan reinforced its air groups in
Indochina with dozens more bombers,

43
00:02:43,404 --> 00:02:46,920
and trained their crews, for
the first time in the war,

44
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,400
specifically in killing
fast ships under way at sea.

45
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:53,651
The arrival, when it came
on the 2nd of December,

46
00:02:53,731 --> 00:02:56,450
was everything the gesture had promised.

47
00:02:56,450 --> 00:02:59,926
The fleet — Prince of Wales,
Repulse and four destroyers —

48
00:03:00,006 --> 00:03:04,832
came up the eastern channel of the Johore
Strait to the naval base at Sembawang,

49
00:03:04,912 --> 00:03:08,020
and General Percival,
commanding the army in Malaya,

50
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:11,822
never forgot it: "I can remember
now the thrill it gave us all

51
00:03:11,902 --> 00:03:15,868
as we watched those majestic ships
steaming up the eastern channel

52
00:03:15,948 --> 00:03:19,750
of the Johore Straits and coming
to anchor at the Naval Base."

53
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:24,250
Singapore's newspapers put the
battleship on the front page.

54
00:03:24,250 --> 00:03:27,750
For a few days, the island felt protected.

55
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:31,045
The man who took command
of the fleet was Admiral

56
00:03:31,125 --> 00:03:34,283
Sir Tom Phillips, appointed
Commander-in-Chief,

57
00:03:34,363 --> 00:03:37,600
Eastern Fleet, in the
very days of the arrival.

58
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:41,785
Phillips was an unlikely sea
dog: a staff officer of genius,

59
00:03:41,865 --> 00:03:46,262
a "desk admiral" who had not held
a major sea command in years,

60
00:03:46,342 --> 00:03:49,825
and a small man — the
fleet called him Tom Thumb.

61
00:03:49,825 --> 00:03:52,498
He was also, by every account, brave,

62
00:03:52,578 --> 00:03:56,150
clever and utterly certain
of his own judgement.

63
00:03:56,150 --> 00:03:58,533
He knew the two captains socially;

64
00:03:58,613 --> 00:04:01,800
Percival dined with all
three men that week.

65
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:05,991
Captain John Leach of Prince of
Wales he remembered as "one of

66
00:04:06,071 --> 00:04:10,606
the finest of men"; Captain William
Tennant of Repulse would prove,

67
00:04:10,686 --> 00:04:15,025
within days, to be one of the
finest ship-handlers in the navy.

68
00:04:15,025 --> 00:04:19,375
War came to Singapore before the
fleet could go looking for it.

69
00:04:19,375 --> 00:04:23,053
In the early hours of the
8th of December, local time,

70
00:04:23,133 --> 00:04:28,342
Japanese bombers raided the island and
Japanese troops landed at Kota Bharu,

71
00:04:28,422 --> 00:04:31,891
in the north of Malaya;
Prince of Wales and Repulse

72
00:04:31,971 --> 00:04:36,425
fired their anti-aircraft guns
at the raiders from their berths.

73
00:04:36,425 --> 00:04:41,213
The news from Hawaii that day killed
the last assumption the plan had

74
00:04:41,293 --> 00:04:45,950
rested on — that the American fleet
might come to Singapore's aid.

75
00:04:45,950 --> 00:04:49,492
Phillips' two ships could
not fight the Japanese navy,

76
00:04:49,572 --> 00:04:51,450
and everyone senior knew it.

77
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:54,868
But Malaya was being
invaded, the invasion convoys

78
00:04:54,948 --> 00:04:58,157
were at sea, and a fleet
that stayed in harbour

79
00:04:58,237 --> 00:05:01,875
while the army fought alone
was no deterrent at all.

80
00:05:01,875 --> 00:05:05,521
That evening, Force Z sailed:
the two great ships and

81
00:05:05,601 --> 00:05:09,247
the destroyers Electra,
Express, Vampire and Tenedos,

82
00:05:09,327 --> 00:05:13,475
steering north to strike the
invasion shipping off Singora.

83
00:05:13,475 --> 00:05:18,400
They sailed handicapped in ways
that never made the communiqués.

84
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:22,446
The flagship's anti-aircraft
fire-control radars had failed

85
00:05:22,526 --> 00:05:26,713
in the tropical heat and humidity,
and RAF technicians ashore

86
00:05:26,793 --> 00:05:30,769
said the repairs would take a
week the fleet did not have;

87
00:05:30,849 --> 00:05:35,325
her two-pounder ammunition had
deteriorated in the same climate.

88
00:05:35,325 --> 00:05:38,167
Phillips asked about fighter cover for the

89
00:05:38,247 --> 00:05:41,725
operation and was told it
could not be guaranteed.

90
00:05:41,725 --> 00:05:44,592
He sailed anyway, in strict radio silence,

91
00:05:44,672 --> 00:05:48,452
counting on surprise and on
weather — a curtain of rain

92
00:05:48,532 --> 00:05:51,900
and cloud under which a
fleet might move unseen.

93
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:54,450
For a day, the curtain held.

94
00:05:54,450 --> 00:05:57,779
Then, at two o'clock on
the afternoon of the 9th,

95
00:05:57,859 --> 00:06:02,093
the Japanese submarine I-65 found
them in a gap in the clouds,

96
00:06:02,173 --> 00:06:06,198
and shadowed them for five
hours, reporting their position;

97
00:06:06,278 --> 00:06:10,800
towards sunset, Japanese cruiser
floatplanes picked up the trail.

98
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:13,394
Phillips did not know he had been seen —

99
00:06:13,474 --> 00:06:17,137
but he knew the arithmetic
of surprise, and that evening

100
00:06:17,217 --> 00:06:20,225
he gave the operation
up and turned for home.

101
00:06:20,225 --> 00:06:23,101
In the darkness the
two fleets, his and the

102
00:06:23,181 --> 00:06:26,470
Japanese covering force,
passed within about five

103
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:29,575
miles of each other,
neither seeing a thing.

104
00:06:29,575 --> 00:06:31,845
Then, fifty minutes past midnight,

105
00:06:31,925 --> 00:06:34,275
came the message that killed them.

106
00:06:34,275 --> 00:06:37,806
A report reached Phillips
— passed on from Singapore

107
00:06:37,886 --> 00:06:41,695
by Palliser — that the Japanese
were landing at Kuantan,

108
00:06:41,775 --> 00:06:44,275
halfway down the coast towards home.

109
00:06:44,275 --> 00:06:47,907
It was exactly the target
his force existed to strike,

110
00:06:47,987 --> 00:06:51,826
and he turned north to strike
it, still in radio silence,

111
00:06:51,906 --> 00:06:54,725
telling no one ashore where he was going.

112
00:06:54,725 --> 00:06:57,100
The report was false.

113
00:06:57,100 --> 00:07:00,750
At dawn his destroyer
Express went in and looked at

114
00:07:00,830 --> 00:07:04,925
Kuantan and found the beaches
empty and the town asleep.

115
00:07:04,925 --> 00:07:08,372
On the way north, at twenty
to four in the morning,

116
00:07:08,452 --> 00:07:14,249
the submarine I-58 had fired five torpedoes
at the force and missed with all of them;

117
00:07:14,329 --> 00:07:17,925
the British never even knew
the attack had happened.

118
00:07:17,925 --> 00:07:21,616
At a quarter past ten that
morning, a Japanese scout

119
00:07:21,696 --> 00:07:25,459
plane found the fleet in
clear sunshine and broadcast

120
00:07:25,539 --> 00:07:28,875
its position to the
bomber bases in Indochina.

121
00:07:28,875 --> 00:07:32,050
The bombers were already on their way.

122
00:07:32,050 --> 00:07:35,852
What followed, from eleven
thirteen on the morning of

123
00:07:35,932 --> 00:07:40,612
the 10th of December 1941, has
been studied by navies ever since,

124
00:07:40,692 --> 00:07:43,475
because it changed what a warship was.

125
00:07:43,475 --> 00:07:47,635
The first wave — eight level
bombers — attacked Repulse alone and

126
00:07:47,715 --> 00:07:51,549
put one bomb through her hangar
into the marines' mess deck;

127
00:07:51,629 --> 00:07:56,000
remarkably, she shrugged it off
and fought on at twenty-five knots.

128
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,764
The torpedo bombers came next, in waves,

129
00:07:58,844 --> 00:08:02,107
the specially trained
ship-killers of three air

130
00:08:02,187 --> 00:08:05,600
groups flying from bases
hundreds of miles away.

131
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:08,285
Repulse, superbly handled by Tennant,

132
00:08:08,365 --> 00:08:12,850
combed the tracks of nineteen
torpedoes and dodged them all.

133
00:08:12,850 --> 00:08:16,075
Prince of Wales was not so lucky.

134
00:08:16,075 --> 00:08:19,173
One torpedo struck her
aft, on the port side,

135
00:08:19,253 --> 00:08:23,058
exactly where the outer
propeller shaft left the hull —

136
00:08:23,138 --> 00:08:26,175
the single most
destructive hit of the day.

137
00:08:26,175 --> 00:08:29,793
The still-spinning shaft tore
its watertight glands apart,

138
00:08:29,873 --> 00:08:32,598
the sea poured into
engine and boiler rooms,

139
00:08:32,678 --> 00:08:35,531
and within minutes the
great ship was listing,

140
00:08:35,611 --> 00:08:39,500
her steering and pumps and much
of her electrical power gone.

141
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:42,920
A Japanese officer watching
from above recorded

142
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,165
his astonishment at the empty
sky: "Much to our surprise,

143
00:08:47,245 --> 00:08:50,075
not a single enemy plane was in sight.

144
00:08:50,075 --> 00:08:53,984
This was all the more amazing
since the scene of battle was

145
00:08:54,064 --> 00:08:57,850
well within the fighting range
of the British fighters."

146
00:08:57,850 --> 00:09:00,295
The fighters were at Sembawang,

147
00:09:00,375 --> 00:09:06,161
ten Brewster Buffaloes of No. 453
Squadron, Royal Australian Air Force —

148
00:09:06,241 --> 00:09:09,825
the squadron designated
to defend the fleet.

149
00:09:09,825 --> 00:09:12,801
Their commander, Flight
Lieutenant Tim Vigors,

150
00:09:12,881 --> 00:09:17,186
had proposed keeping aircraft
standing over the fleet in daylight;

151
00:09:17,266 --> 00:09:19,525
the proposal had been turned down.

152
00:09:19,525 --> 00:09:23,291
In the event, no call for
help left the flagship at all:

153
00:09:23,371 --> 00:09:26,325
Phillips kept his radio
silence to the end.

154
00:09:26,325 --> 00:09:30,189
It was Tennant, in Repulse,
who finally broke silence and

155
00:09:30,269 --> 00:09:34,075
asked for the fighters — an
hour after the first bombs.

156
00:09:34,075 --> 00:09:36,850
By then his ship was dying.

157
00:09:36,850 --> 00:09:40,535
At about twenty past twelve
the last torpedo wave caught

158
00:09:40,615 --> 00:09:44,771
Repulse in a pincer, aircraft
attacking from both sides at once

159
00:09:44,851 --> 00:09:48,738
so that dodging one spread of
torpedoes turned her into the

160
00:09:48,818 --> 00:09:52,650
path of another; four or five
struck home within minutes.

161
00:09:52,650 --> 00:09:56,247
Tennant ordered his men
over the side, and at twelve

162
00:09:56,327 --> 00:09:59,650
thirty-three Repulse
rolled over and went down.

163
00:09:59,650 --> 00:10:02,849
Prince of Wales, hit by
three more torpedoes and then

164
00:10:02,929 --> 00:10:06,500
bombed again — one bomb bursting
among the wounded gathered

165
00:10:06,580 --> 00:10:09,284
in her cinema flat —
held on a little longer,

166
00:10:09,364 --> 00:10:13,491
the destroyer Express alongside her
taking off the wounded while the

167
00:10:13,571 --> 00:10:16,894
flagship heeled over so far
that her bilge keel scraped

168
00:10:16,974 --> 00:10:20,625
down the destroyer's side and
nearly dragged her under too.

169
00:10:20,625 --> 00:10:23,850
At one eighteen, the flagship sank.

170
00:10:23,850 --> 00:10:26,838
At one eighteen, at that very minute,

171
00:10:26,918 --> 00:10:32,225
the Buffaloes of 453 Squadron
arrived over the oil and wreckage.

172
00:10:32,225 --> 00:10:34,924
Vigors flew low over the men in the water,

173
00:10:35,004 --> 00:10:37,969
and what he saw went
into the official record:

174
00:10:38,049 --> 00:10:42,733
they "waved and put up his thumb as
I flew over him… like holiday-makers

175
00:10:42,813 --> 00:10:45,725
at Brighton waving at
a low-flying aircraft.

176
00:10:45,725 --> 00:10:49,875
It shook me, for here was
something above human nature."

177
00:10:49,875 --> 00:10:52,500
The destroyers did the rest.

178
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:56,543
Electra and Vampire worked through
Repulse's men while Express

179
00:10:56,623 --> 00:11:00,334
took off the flagship's;
Electra alone pulled 571 men out

180
00:11:00,414 --> 00:11:04,257
of the sea, and Repulse's
survivors — fished out of the oil

181
00:11:04,337 --> 00:11:08,048
half an hour before — manned
Electra's after guns so that

182
00:11:08,128 --> 00:11:10,774
her own sailors could stay at the rescue,

183
00:11:10,854 --> 00:11:15,975
while Repulse's dentist worked beside
Electra's medical teams on the wounded.

184
00:11:15,975 --> 00:11:19,500
In all, 2,081 men were saved.

185
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:23,989
Eight hundred and forty were
not: 513 from Repulse and 327

186
00:11:24,069 --> 00:11:28,794
from Prince of Wales, among them
Captain Leach, who went down

187
00:11:28,874 --> 00:11:33,600
with his ship, and Admiral
Phillips, who went down with his.

188
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:36,771
Tennant survived, pulled
from the water by Vampire,

189
00:11:36,851 --> 00:11:40,531
and when the survivors were
landed in Singapore that night,

190
00:11:40,611 --> 00:11:44,865
the air commander, Air Vice-Marshal
Pulford, met him with the words:

191
00:11:44,945 --> 00:11:47,750
"My God, I hope you
don't blame me for this.

192
00:11:47,750 --> 00:11:50,575
I had no idea where you were."

193
00:11:50,575 --> 00:11:54,103
The next day, a Japanese
pilot, Lieutenant Haruki Iki,

194
00:11:54,183 --> 00:11:57,912
flew back alone to the place
where the ships had sunk and

195
00:11:57,992 --> 00:12:01,586
dropped two wreaths into the
sea — one for his own dead

196
00:12:01,666 --> 00:12:05,475
of the Kanoya Air Group, and
one for the British sailors.

197
00:12:05,475 --> 00:12:09,997
The shock reached London by telephone,
at the Prime Minister's bedside,

198
00:12:10,077 --> 00:12:13,822
from the First Sea Lord, Sir
Dudley Pound: "Prime Minister,

199
00:12:13,902 --> 00:12:17,452
I have to report to you that
the Prince of Wales and the

200
00:12:17,532 --> 00:12:21,875
Repulse have both been sunk by the
Japanese — we think by aircraft.

201
00:12:21,875 --> 00:12:24,525
Tom Phillips is drowned."

202
00:12:24,525 --> 00:12:28,680
Churchill wrote afterwards: "In
all the war, I never received

203
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:32,568
a more direct shock… Across
this vast expanse of waters,

204
00:12:32,648 --> 00:12:36,675
Japan was supreme, and we
everywhere were weak and naked."

205
00:12:36,675 --> 00:12:40,090
The two ships were the first
capital ships in history,

206
00:12:40,170 --> 00:12:43,262
steaming and fighting in
the open sea, to be sunk

207
00:12:43,342 --> 00:12:46,563
by air power alone —
eighty-eight Japanese aircraft

208
00:12:46,643 --> 00:12:49,750
had done it, for the loss
of three of their own.

209
00:12:49,750 --> 00:12:53,389
Singapore, built and boasted
of as a naval fortress,

210
00:12:53,469 --> 00:12:56,108
was now a land fortress with no fleet,

211
00:12:56,188 --> 00:13:00,050
which was the one thing it
had never been meant to be.

212
00:13:00,050 --> 00:13:04,215
Nine weeks later, on the
15th of February 1942,

213
00:13:04,295 --> 00:13:05,650
it surrendered.

214
00:13:05,650 --> 00:13:07,950
The ships are still there.

215
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:12,499
Both wrecks lie upside down in
the South China Sea off Kuantan —

216
00:13:12,579 --> 00:13:15,827
Repulse in 56 metres of
water, Prince of Wales

217
00:13:15,907 --> 00:13:18,937
in 68 — and they are
designated war graves,

218
00:13:19,017 --> 00:13:23,567
the property of the Crown, with
buoys riding over them and White

219
00:13:23,647 --> 00:13:27,625
Ensigns on the buoy lines
that Royal Navy divers renew.

220
00:13:27,625 --> 00:13:30,595
Every Royal Navy ship that passes holds

221
00:13:30,675 --> 00:13:33,725
a service of remembrance over the site.

222
00:13:33,725 --> 00:13:38,315
But a war grave made of tens of
thousands of tons of pre-war steel —

223
00:13:38,395 --> 00:13:42,298
steel smelted before the first
atomic bombs filled the air

224
00:13:42,378 --> 00:13:46,625
with background radiation, and
therefore valuable for sensitive

225
00:13:46,705 --> 00:13:51,375
scientific and medical instruments
— is a prize as well as a shrine.

226
00:13:51,375 --> 00:13:56,102
In 2002 an authorised team
brought up Prince of Wales's bell,

227
00:13:56,182 --> 00:13:59,018
ahead of the looters; it is displayed

228
00:13:59,098 --> 00:14:03,275
today at the Merseyside
Maritime Museum in Liverpool.

229
00:14:03,275 --> 00:14:06,940
In 2014 the wrecks were
reported extensively

230
00:14:07,020 --> 00:14:11,275
damaged by explosives used
by scrap-metal dealers.

231
00:14:11,275 --> 00:14:14,666
And in May 2023 the Malaysian
authorities detained a

232
00:14:14,746 --> 00:14:18,805
Fuzhou-registered salvage vessel
anchored illegally off Johor,

233
00:14:18,885 --> 00:14:22,543
and found aboard her scrap
metal and more than a hundred

234
00:14:22,623 --> 00:14:26,548
unexploded artillery shells,
believed to have been scavenged

235
00:14:26,628 --> 00:14:30,500
from the two wrecks; thirty-two
of her crew were detained.

236
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:35,294
Britain's National Museum of the
Royal Navy spoke for the dead:

237
00:14:35,374 --> 00:14:39,781
"We are distressed and concerned
at the apparent vandalism

238
00:14:39,861 --> 00:14:44,425
for personal profit of HMS
Prince of Wales and HMS Repulse.

239
00:14:44,425 --> 00:14:47,350
They are designated war graves."

240
00:14:47,350 --> 00:14:49,752
And the remembering goes on,

241
00:14:49,832 --> 00:14:54,175
in forms the men of 1941
could not have imagined.

242
00:14:54,175 --> 00:14:58,854
In September 2025 the Royal Navy's
new aircraft carrier — named,

243
00:14:58,934 --> 00:15:04,281
by a coincidence the service does not
treat as one, HMS Prince of Wales —

244
00:15:04,361 --> 00:15:08,525
stopped over the wrecks and
held a committal of wreaths.

245
00:15:08,525 --> 00:15:12,728
Beside the British officers at
the rail stood a young officer of

246
00:15:12,808 --> 00:15:16,944
the Japanese destroyer Akebono,
Sub Lieutenant Takumi Kitamura,

247
00:15:17,024 --> 00:15:21,561
helping to honour the same dead:
the navies of the two countries that

248
00:15:21,641 --> 00:15:26,125
fought over those ships, laying
wreaths together in the same water.

249
00:15:26,125 --> 00:15:30,193
Eighty-four years after Singapore
thrilled to a fleet that was

250
00:15:30,273 --> 00:15:34,006
meant to frighten an enemy
away, the ships' real garrison

251
00:15:34,086 --> 00:15:38,300
turns out to be memory — and
unlike the deterrent, it has held.
